            <div xml:id="d661">

                <pb facs="0610" n="603" xml:id="pg603"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.20/632: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-10-12T15:00:00+08:00">October 12, 1937—3
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received October 12—10:43 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>801. With reference to Shanghai’s 805, October 4, 3 p.m., I took advantage of an
                    opportunity offered in a conversation which I had with Chang Chun who now holds
                    important position in War Activities Council as Secretary General, and with Vice
                    Minister Hsu Mo, to express the hope that the Chinese Government would not make
                    the mistake of using American or other foreign aviators to take large bombing
                    planes to Japan for the purpose of demonstrating their ability to bomb Japanese
                    cities. I was assured that they knew of no such scheme. I urged them to dissuade
                    anyone in the Government who might be advocating such a plan.</p>
                <p>Repeated to Shanghai.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
